is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the physical/data-plane link from leaf_router_0_0 to spine_router_0_1, specifically interface eth1 on leaf_router_0_0 where the eBGP peering to 172.16.0.8 should run. FRR on leaf_router_0_0 shows the neighbor 172.16.0.8 in Active state (show ip bgp summary), indicating TCP cannot establish. On the same device, the OS reports the interface is not operational: ip addr shows eth1 in state DOWN and ethtool reports “Link detected: no.” In contrast, the remote side (spine_router_0_1) has eth1 up with carrier (ip addr state UP; ethtool “Link detected: yes”), so the failure localizes to the leaf side of the link.

This explains the symptom pattern: with the eth1 link down, the BGP session to spine_router_0_1 cannot form, so leaf_router_0_0 forwards via its remaining uplink to spine_router_0_0; end-to-end traffic between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 continues, but redundancy is lost and monitoring flags the missing session.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_0’s interface eth1 toward spine_router_0_1 was administratively shut or lost carrier, breaking the L2/L3 adjacency and the BGP session.

Corrective fix: On leaf_router_0_0, bring the interface up (ip link set dev eth1 up) and verify carrier (ethtool eth1). Confirm the BGP session establishes (show ip bgp summary) and routes are exchanged. For persistence, ensure no startup script or config leaves eth1 down and that the interface addressing and neighbor statements match (172.16.0.9/31 ↔ 172.16.0.8/31). === END ===